 **Parameters:**
-*   **Length Threshold:** A minimum acceptable length for a particle trace. Initially set to a large value, then gradually reduced to minimum [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Constant Time Interval:** Used to determine segment length based on vector magnitude [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Stagnation Radius ($r$):** The radius of the dot when velocity approaches zero [S1].
+*   $\Delta t$: Constant time interval for segment length calculation. Not given in the sources.
+*   $L_{thresh}$: Length threshold. Initially set to a large value, then gradually reduced to minimum [S1].
+*   $L_{min}$: Minimum length threshold. Not given in the sources.
 
 **Pitfalls:**
-*   **Directional Ambiguity:** In still images, lines may appear bidirectional. This is resolved by using a sawtooth-shaped color map across each segment [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Recalculation Cost:** Erasing short traces can be expensive if not optimized. Preserving the length of erased traces in the cells they passed through prevents extensive recalculation during backtracking [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Viewpoint Dependence:** Viewpoint-dependent projection techniques require particle traces to be recalculated when the viewpoint changes, whereas viewpoint-independent techniques (normal, xy normal, cylinder) do not [S1].
+*   Directional ambiguity in still images is resolved using a sawtooth-shaped color map across each segment [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   Random seed placement does not guarantee coverage near every point on the surfaces [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Vector Field Projection onto Surface](13034-vector-field-projection-onto-surface.md)
-Techniques for projecting a 3D vector onto a 2D surface to determine the direction of particle trace segments. Includes normal, xy normal, cylinder, and eye projections.
+This family defines methods for projecting a 3D vector field onto a 2D surface to determine the direction of particle trace segments, as required by [Particle Traces on 2D Surfaces](12772-particle-traces-on-2d-surfaces.md). The primary distinction among siblings is viewpoint dependency: most techniques are viewpoint independent, while the vieweye projection requires recalculation when the viewpoint changes.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of seed points, the length of traces, and the threshold reduction strategy. Gradually reducing the threshold from maximum to minimum produces the most aesthetically pleasing distribution but takes longer to calculate than using a single length threshold value [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The octree structure is much more memory efficient than a full 3D grid, storing only cells that intersect the surfaces [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On an SGI Onyx workstation, real-time rotation and particle motion can be achieved on small data sets, and interactive performance on large data sets, using hardware texture mapping [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Guarantees coverage near every point on the surface (unlike random seeding); keeps line density nearly constant; allows long towlines to be easily understood; avoids density issues [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Viewpoint-dependent projections require recalculation; gradual threshold reduction is computationally expensive; surfaces must not pass through any cell twice [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. However, the method involves hierarchical seed selection and advection, which may be computationally expensive for large surfaces and complex vector fields. Gradually reducing the threshold increases computation time compared to using a single threshold [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The octree representation is more memory efficient than a full 3D grid, storing only cells intersecting the surfaces [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Real-time rotation and particle motion can be achieved on small data sets, and interactive performance on large data sets, using hardware texture mapping on an Onyx2 workstation [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Guarantees coverage near every point on the surface, avoids density issues, allows for long flowlines to be easily understood, and supports color table animation for motion [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Viewpoint dependent projection techniques require recalculation of particle traces when the viewpoint changes [S1]. Gradually reducing the threshold takes longer to calculate [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was developed to visualize 3D velocity fields near contour surfaces, allowing long flowlines to be easily understood without becoming too dense or sparse [S1][S2, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of color table animation for flow visualization, an old technique [Shoup79] applied to flow visualization by Van Gelder and Wilhelms [VanGelder92] [S1]. The method was implemented and tested on an SGI Onyx workstation [S2, unofficial].
+The technique was introduced in the paper "Visualizing 3D velocity fields near contour surfaces" by Van Gelder and Wilhelms [S1][S2, unofficial]. The authors experimented with several different projections of the vector field to the surfaces, including normal, xy normal, cylinder, and eye projections [S1][S2, unofficial]. Color table animation, an old technique [Shoup79], was applied to flow visualization by Van Gelder and Wilhelms [VanGelder92] and was a primary goal of this technique [S1].
 
